meta {
  name: Connect
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseURL}}/google-calendar/connect/
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Accept: application/json
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
}

docs {
  Returns Google's consent-screen URL as JSON ({"data": {"auth_url": "..."}}).
  The frontend navigates the browser there itself.

  No body needed — this no longer names which associate is connecting, and
  there's no `state` param either; identity is worked out later in
  /complete/ from the connected Google account's own verified email
  (matched against Associate.user.email). Whichever Google account the
  staff member actually signs into on the consent screen decides who gets
  connected — not whoever called /connect/.

  Method: POST
  Path: /google-calendar/connect/
  Source: integrations.google_calendar.views.GoogleCalendarConnectView
  Auth: None — authentication_classes = [] (this view never reads request.user; identity is resolved in /complete/ instead, see above, so IsAssociateUser can't come back here without also removing authentication_classes = []).

  Manual end-to-end flow (no real frontend running locally):
  1. Run this request. Copy data.auth_url from the response.
  2. Paste auth_url into a real browser, signed in as the Google account
     you want to connect. Approve consent.
  3. Google redirects to DEV_GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI, which itself redirects to
     ADMIN_FRONTEND_URL/#code=... (a URL fragment, not a query string — still
     visible in the address bar). The page itself may 404 (no frontend
     needed) — just copy `code` out of the address bar.
  4. Paste it into this environment's google_oauth_code var, then run
     Complete.bru.
}
